196 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 117 0 R
/E (Originally known as Murumdev, Rajgad Fort existed long before the Maratha era. Around 1490 CE, the Nizam Shahi dynasty took control of it peacefully, after which Ahmad Bahiri demolished its temples and built mosques in their place. The fort remained under Nizam Shahi control for nearly 125 years. For much of this period, hill forts had fallen out of favour as ruling dynasties preferred governing from plains cities like Ahmadnagar and Bijapur. This changed in the early 1600s when Malik Ambar revived their military value against Mughal and Adil Shahi invasions, relocating his administrative centre near Daulatabad by 1610 CE. Around 1625 CE, Murumdev briefly passed to the Adil Shahi dynasty when its keepers surrendered on Malik Ambar's orders, though this occupation did not last long. By 1630 CE it returned to Nizam Shahi control, largely due to Shahaji's influence across the Maval region. When Bijapur forces attacked, the fort was bravely defended by Shahaji's officer Sonji and reinforced by Walaji Naik Shilimkar, who repulsed the assault despite being wounded. Shahaji personally honoured Walaji for his courage, highlighting the fort's renewed strategic importance.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
