219 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 119 0 R
/E (Rising 1,376 metres above the Sahyadri ranges, Rajgad is not merely a mountain fortress it is the physical embodiment of a political vision. Known originally as Murumdev, the fort had passed through Nizam Shahi and Adil Shahi hands before Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj captured it in 1647 CE. His first act was to rename it: Murumdev became Rajgad, the King's Fort. This was no formality it was a declaration carved into the mountain's identity, marked by a royal crown worked in stone above the arch of the Balekilla gateway.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
220 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 119 0 R
/T (The First Capital of Swarajya)
/E (The First Capital of Swarajya)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
221 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 119 0 R
/E (Within months of capture, construction was underway. By 1662 CE, royal residences, administrative halls, granaries, temples, and a garrison had all risen from the basalt ridge. A pradhanmandal a council of ministers was established here from the earliest years. Coins were minted at Rajgad, asserting economic sovereignty as real as any military victory. Legal disputes were adjudicated above the clouds, and every major event of the formative years of Swarajya passed through this fort. When the Treaty of Purandar in 1665 forced the cession of twenty-three forts to the Mughals, Shivaji negotiated with extraordinary care to )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
222 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 119 0 R
/E (retain Rajgad losing the capital would cost not just land but institutional memory and symbolic legitimacy.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
223 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 119 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
224 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 119 0 R
/E (In the context of territorial expansion in the Maval region, both Rajgad and Torna emerged as critical forts. 3 Although Torna was considered impregnable, its balekilla \(citadel\) was relatively small and unsuitable as a royal administrative centre. In contrast, Rajgad offered greater seclusion, a more expansive balekilla, and was naturally defended by rivers or streams on all approach routes; qualities that made it far more secure. 4 It is for these reasons that Shivaji Maharaj chose Rajgad as his first royal capital.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
225 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 119 0 R
/E (Maval forms an important component of the larger Ghatmatha and held considerable strategic value during the medieval period. Numerous forts were established to command and secure its terrain. 18 The belts of Gunjan, Kanad and Velvand fall within the surveillance zone of Sinhagad, Torna and Rajgad. Rajgad in particular became the first capital of Shivaji Maharaj precisely because this region offered exceptional natural strength and strategic depth.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
