/Pg 18 0 R
/A [616 0 R 0]
/K [6]
>>
endobj
237 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 120 0 R
/E (In June 1665, Shivaji signed the Treaty of Purandar with Jai Singh, ceding twenty-three forts to the Mughals while retaining twelve. He strategically ensured Rajgad was among those kept, along with Torna, Lingana, and Rajgad, which together formed a continuous defensive chain within the same mountain range. During his absence, the official Keso Narayan exploited the lack of a havaldar or sarnobat to commit embezzlement. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
238 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 120 0 R
/E (After his famous escape from Agra, Shivaji arrived safely at Rajgad on 12 September 1666. His son Sambhaji followed on 20 November 1666, escorted by Krishnaji Vishwasrao, whom Shivaji generously rewarded for his efforts.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
239 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 120 0 R
/E (Rajaram, Shivaji's younger son, was born at Rajgad on 24 February 1670, and it is widely believed he was named after the fort itself. That same year, Shivaji dispatched Tanaji Malusare from Rajgad to recapture Sinhagad. News of Tanaji's successful but fatal assault reached Shivaji at Rajgad via a gari \(fire beacon\) lit atop the conquered fort. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
