/E (THE MUGHAL YEARS)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
248 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 121 0 R
/E (Following Shivaji's death in 1680 and Sambhaji's capture and execution in 1689, Mughal commander Kishorsing Hada seized Rajgad in June 1689. The Mughal historian Khafi Khan amusingly records how the newly appointed kiladar \(fort commander\) Abul Kher Khan panicked when surrounded by Maratha forces, attempted a cowardly night escape with his family in dolis \(palanquins\), and was intercepted and thoroughly humiliated by the waiting Marathas.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
249 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 121 0 R
/E (Contemporary Mughal chroniclers underline Rajgad's exceptional height, scale, and near-impregnability. Saki Mustaid Khan \(Maasir-i-Alamgiri\) and Khafi Khan \(Muntakhab-al-Lubab\) describe its twelve-kos perimeter, the arduous ascent through forests and ravines, and the strength of Shivaji's three Machis.6 Mustaid Khan adds specific observations: the Sond ramparts shaped like an elephant's trunk, rising above deep gorges, and a main bastion thirty gaz high. After finally capturing the fort in 1704, the Mughals renamed it Navishahugad in an attempt to impose symbolic authority over a stronghold they had long considered unattainable.7)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
250 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 121 0 R
/T (CHAPTER 04)
/E (CHAPTER 04)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [6]
>>
