/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 125 0 R
/T (Rajgad Fort is as remarkable for its architecture as it is for its history. Perched on a central cone rising to approximately 1,376 metres \(4,514 ft\), the fort showcases a sophisticated multi-tiered design that integrates military construction with its dramatic Sahyadri terrain. Its layout is divided into four interconnected sections, the central Balekilla \(citadel\) and three expansive sub-plateaus or Machis, giving it a distinctive tricorn shape with a total circumference of around 40 kilometres.)
/E (Rajgad Fort is as remarkable for its architecture as it is for its history. Perched on a central cone rising to approximately 1,376 metres \(4,514 ft\), the fort showcases a sophisticated multi-tiered design that integrates military construction with its dramatic Sahyadri terrain. Its layout is divided into four interconnected sections, the central Balekilla \(citadel\) and three expansive sub-plateaus or Machis, giving it a distinctive tricorn shape with a total circumference of around 40 kilometres.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
278 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 125 0 R
/T (The Balekilla forms the highest point of the fort, offering commanding 360-degree views of neighbouring forts including Torna and Sinhagad. Radiating from it are the three Machis, each functioning as an independent sub-fort with its own military and civilian infrastructure, overseen by a Sarnaubat. The Padmavati Machi, the largest, served as the administrative and residential centre and houses the Padmavati Temple and the fort's primary water source. The Sanjeevani Machi to the southwest is notable for its multi-layered, triple-line fortification, while the Suvela Machi, a long eastern ridge, features the natural rock hole known as the Nedhe. Rainwater was collected into cisterns and natural tanks across the Machis, essential for sustaining the fort as a capital.)
/E (The Balekilla forms the highest point of the fort, offering commanding 360-degree views of neighbouring forts including Torna and Sinhagad. Radiating from it are the three Machis, each functioning as an independent sub-fort with its own military and civilian infrastructure, overseen by a Sarnaubat. The Padmavati Machi, the largest, served as the administrative and residential centre and houses the Padmavati Temple and the fort's primary water source. The Sanjeevani Machi to the southwest is notable for its multi-layered, triple-line fortification, while the Suvela Machi, a long eastern ridge, features the natural rock hole known as the Nedhe. Rainwater was collected into cisterns and natural tanks across the Machis, essential for sustaining the fort as a capital.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
