306 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 128 0 R
/E (The Balekilla is the crown of Rajgad at 1,394 metres the highest point, the final refuge, the administrative and symbolic centre. Its triangular perimeter wall traces every contour of the basalt summit with obsessive topographic precision, and its single gateway narrows to force any attacker into single file. Within, the most sensitive functions of the Maratha state were housed: the palace complex, the Sadar administrative office, the grain store, the gunpowder magazine, sardar residences, and two temples. All of this survives today only as foundation courses and wall stubs but to stand within them is to stand where the first ministry of Swarajya convened, where the first coins of an independent Maratha state were authorised.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
307 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 128 0 R
/E (The Balekilla, the crowning feature of Rajgad Fort, reaches an elevation of 1,394 metres above sea level. Situated atop a narrow and steep hill, the citadel's demanding ascent rewards climbers with stunning views and historical significance. The fort's layout incorporates a secure triangular perimeter, reinforced by robust stone walls that align with the irregular contours of the summit, offering both strategic defence and a commanding perspective of the surrounding landscape.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
308 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 128 0 R
/E (Visitors access the Balekilla via stone-carved steps that lead up a narrow path bordered by a deep valley on one side and a steep cliff on the other. To ensure safety, iron railings are installed along the valley side. Upon reaching the summit, climbers encounter the imposing gate of the citadel.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
309 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 128 0 R
/E (The main entrance to the Balekilla is the Gateway \(G14\), which serves as the sole controlled access point. This strategically designed choke point is protected by two bastions, forming a multi-layered defence system aimed at thwarting invaders. The entrance passage is deliberately narrow, measuring approximately 1.05 metres in width, creating a constricted space that disadvantages any would-be attackers.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
310 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 128 0 R
/E (The Balekilla gate is structurally sound, constructed from dense ashlar masonry and features pointed arches. Robust octagonal bastions flank the gate, which stands at about 5.4 metres tall. Evidence of the original wooden doors can still be seen, marked by pivot holes and fastening marks. Hollow recesses within the passage likely accommodated removable barriers for additional defence. The gateway is adorned with Islamic-style canopies on either side, reminiscent of the gate at Raigad.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
311 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 128 0 R
/E (The Balekilla's interior features a markedly undulating topography, with its south-central zone forming the highest point and housing the principal residential structures. Natural depressions between steep landforms were adapted into water storage lakes. During its time as Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj's first capital, the Balekilla contained residential, administrative, and religious buildings alongside major water bodies.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
312 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 128 0 R
/E (Behind the Brahmatirth Temple, a north-to-south pathway once lined with shops on both sides suggests Rajgad contained a designated commercial zone, though smaller and less elaborate than the better-preserved marketplace at Padmavati Machi. The complex appears to have comprised around five or six shops on each side of a central road approximately 4.5 metres wide, each unit likely including a front sales room and a rear storage room. Some scholars, however, argue these ruins may represent soldier accommodation rather than a bazaar, considering it unlikely that a formal market would be located within the Balekilla, which served primarily as an emergency refuge. The advanced state of ruin makes definitive identification difficult.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
313 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 128 0 R
/E (The summit's most significant ruin is the palace complex of Shivaji Maharaj at the highest southern-central point of the citadel. Although no vertical elevations survive, ashlar masonry outlines three distinct structures aligned east to west on a shared plinth. Other associated ruins include the Sadar \(administrative office\), Mudapakh Khana \(kitchen\), sardars' residences, Ambarkhana, Darukothar, and several other structures whose functions remain uncertain due to advanced decay.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
314 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 128 0 R
/E (The Balekilla's water management is anchored by the Chandrakor Talav, a rock-cut cistern adjacent to the Brahmatirth Temple that likely served as the summit's primary water source. Historical references indicate that at least two additional water tanks once existed within the citadel complex.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
315 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 128 0 R
/E (Rajgad's most significant archaeological remains are concentrated on the Padmavati Machi and the Balekilla, where ruins of residential, civic, and military structures from the Maratha period survive in varying states of preservation. While some structures have been restored, key ruins including the Kacheri and Wada on the Padmavati Machi, and the royal Rajwada and market atop the Balekilla, remain in a dilapidated condition. Iconographically, the fort's remains are relatively sparse. The bastions occasionally feature low-relief carvings of protective and auspicious deities, principally Ganesha and Hanuman, which are actively venerated by visitors today and frequently adorned with sindoor. The principal sculptural remains are the four-armed image of Devi Padmavati within the Padmavati Temple, and the Shivalinga and Nandi image at the Brahmarishi Kunda atop the Suvela Machi.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
