/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 129 0 R
/E (In 1660 CE, during Siddi Johar's siege of Panhala, Jijabai was stationed at Rajgad and swiftly dispatched Netaji Palkar and Siddi Hilal to break the siege. That same year, Aurangzeb ordered Shaista Khan to invade Maratha territories. Persian records confirm that Shaista Khan's forces ravaged villages near Rajgad but did not attempt a direct siege of the fort.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
322 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 129 0 R
/E (By 1662 CE, construction work was actively underway at Rajgad. A letter from Shivaji Maharaj dated 13 July 1662 reveals the fort's role as a place of punishment, where he threatened a local Deshmukh and his associates with imprisonment and forced labour carrying stones. After his famous surprise raid on Shaista Khan on 6 April 1663, Shivaji immediately returned to Rajgad.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
323 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 129 0 R
/E (In 1665 CE, Mirza Raja Jai Singh led a Mughal expedition with a strategy to blockade multiple forts simultaneously. Daud Khan and Raisinh were sent towards Rohida and Rajgad, burning surrounding villages to spread terror. On 30 April 1665, the Mughal army launched a direct assault on Rajgad, but the Maratha garrison resisted fiercely and forced a full retreat. )
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
324 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 129 0 R
/E (The Padmavati Machi is the most strategically and politically significant of Rajgad's three spurs. Unlike the other Machis, it served a dual purpose, functioning as both a military outpost and the principal residential and administrative centre of the fort. Standing at 1,195 metres, it is the highest of the three Machis, and its major fortifications were completed in 1662 CE after four years of construction.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
