/P 131 0 R
/E (The Sanjeevani Machi is Rajgad's most sophisticated defensive spur, commissioned by Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj and stretching approximately 2 kilometres along the natural southwestern ridge. Its defining feature is a triple-segmented construction where each tier culminates in a robust bastion at the western end, maximising the use of the narrow tapering topography. The entire length is secured by a multi-layered rampart system of high-quality stone masonry, with at least nineteen bastions \(including some tiered\), nine of which are reinforced with inner chilkhati buruj \(layered bastions\).)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
348 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 131 0 R
/E (The northern chain begins with bastions B4, B5, and B6, which are tilheri buruj \(triple-layered bastions\) rising over 25 metres. The lowermost layer encloses the base of the larger internal bastion, while the upper fortifications show some damage. Further along, bastions B10, B11, and B12 feature impressively tall ashlar masonry built against the vertical ridge. The Machi culminates at Bastion B13, the southwestern sentinel of Rajgad, which integrates natural rocky outcrop with structural masonry to form a towering complex rising 37.28 metres, accessible via a dedicated gateway G18.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
349 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 131 0 R
/E (The southern fortifications mirror the northern chain, beginning with chilkhati bastions B14, B15, and B16. Near B16 lies the Alu Darwaza \(G17\), a small arched gateway providing access from Male village. The innermost and largest bastion is B8, locally known as the Vyaghra Buruj \(Tiger Bastion\), entered via an arched gateway G20. Within the third tier, a bhuyari parakota \(subterranean defence passage\) connected to the outer wall by hidden steps provided both secure shelter for troops and the capability for surprise counterattacks. Adjacent bastion B9 features loopholes for discharging projectiles.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
