358 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 132 0 R
/E (After capturing Murumdev, Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj fortified the eastern spur of Rajgad and named it Suvela Machi, meaning "auspicious time," reflecting its orientation towards the rising sun. Narrow and elongated compared to the other Machis, Suvela Machi forms a long strategic spur extending southeast from the Balekilla, with formidable stone ramparts adapted to its irregular natural topography. The Machi is laid out in three segments. The first begins with a small hillock called Duba, beyond which lie the ruins of residences historically attributed to key officers including Tanhaji Malusare, Yesaji Kank, Shilimkar, and the Sarnobat, each with nearby water facilities indicating self-sufficiency.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
359 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 132 0 R
/E (The main entrance, the Kaleshwari or Didi Darwaza, exploits the rugged valley terrain to create a formidable approach. Secondary access points include the G9 gateway, the Male Darwaza \(G3\), and G11. The fortification employs a tiered system using natural stone slopes as foundations, punctuated by a sequence of bastions including B21 to B24. Of the seventeen bastions along the Machi, seven are of the chilkhati buruj type, demonstrating a deliberate strategy of layered defence. Bastions B19 and B20 are heavily ruined, while B22, known as the Kaleshwari Bastion, is better preserved with an oval layout offering superior surveillance. B23 is a major south-facing two-tier chilkhati buruj.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
360 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 132 0 R
/T (The most striking feature of Suvela Machi is the eastern ridge, approached via the commanding cylindrical Tehalni Buruj \(Watch Bastion\), bastion B25, which rises 12 metres and is built upon a natural rock formation resembling a colossal east-facing elephant, locally known as the Hatti Prastar. Within this formation is the famous Nedhe or Needle Hole Rock, a natural cavity window popular among trekkers and climbers. The second segment culminates in bastion B26, the eastern sentinel of Rajgad, featuring double tiers of fortification built onto the rocky outcrop, though now significantly deteriorated. Both segments of Suvela Machi feature outer parakotas \(protective walls\) with jharokhas \(windows\) for offensive use, along with an underground bhuyari parakota \(underground passage\) set half a metre inward from the outer wall, connected to the exterior by a hidden stairway allowing soldiers to reach firing positions safely. Water management was central to Suvela Machi's strategic planning. Several major cisterns, designated W15 to W19, were constructed to sustain the garrison along the spur. One of these, WT16, is locally known as the Brahmarishi Kunda and doubles as a Rameshwar Temple, housing a Shivalinga, a broken Nandi \(bull\), a female idol, and a male figure possibly representing Veerbhadra or a Dvarapala \(door guardian\). )
/E (The most striking feature of Suvela Machi is the eastern ridge, approached via the commanding cylindrical Tehalni Buruj \(Watch Bastion\), bastion B25, which rises 12 metres and is built upon a natural rock formation resembling a colossal east-facing elephant, locally known as the Hatti Prastar. Within this formation is the famous Nedhe or Needle Hole Rock, a natural cavity window popular among trekkers and climbers. The second segment culminates in bastion B26, the eastern sentinel of Rajgad, featuring double tiers of fortification built onto the rocky outcrop, though now significantly deteriorated. Both segments of Suvela Machi feature outer parakotas \(protective walls\) with jharokhas \(windows\) for offensive use, along with an underground bhuyari parakota \(underground passage\) set half a metre inward from the outer wall, connected to the exterior by a hidden stairway allowing soldiers to reach firing positions safely. Water management was central to Suvela Machi's strategic planning. Several major cisterns, designated W15 to W19, were constructed to sustain the garrison along the spur. One of these, WT16, is locally known as the Brahmarishi Kunda and doubles as a Rameshwar Temple, housing a Shivalinga, a broken Nandi \(bull\), a female idol, and a male figure possibly representing Veerbhadra or a Dvarapala \(door guardian\). )
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [7]
>>
