381 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 134 0 R
/E (Where the Pali approach is the King's Road broad, dignified, built for the passage of state the Gunjavane approach is something rawer. The ascent from the northeast is brutally steep, with narrow stairways and foothold niches carved directly into near-vertical rock. The Gunjavane Darwaza is a sequence of three successive gateways the longest gateway complex on the fort. The second gate carries an intricately carved sculpture possibly Maya Devi or Lakshmi that predates Shivaji's occupation by centuries, likely originating under the Rashtrakutas or Western Chalukyas. When you pass through it, you cross a threshold potentially a thousand years old.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
382 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 134 0 R
/T (Pali Darwaza 2 \(Mukhya Darwaza\))
/E (Pali Darwaza 2 \(Mukhya Darwaza\))
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
383 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 134 0 R
/E (Around 200 metres beyond the first gate lies Pali Darwaza 2, locally known as the Mukhya Darwaza. Larger than the first, it features a robust pointed arch entrance flanked by ashlar masonry bastions equipped with merlons and arrowslits. A defining feature is the pair of rounded projecting balconies called phalikas, designed for operating small cannons, with petal-shaped merlons concealing defenders' movements. Smaller loopholes beneath the parapet allowed attacks from covered positions. At the centre of the gateway is a square surveillance window, and above the arch sits a carved royal crown, likely commissioned under Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj as a symbol of sovereign authority. Beyond the gate, arcaded guardrooms on both the north and south sides monitored incoming and internal movement.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
384 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 134 0 R
/T (Gunjavane Darwaza )
/E (Gunjavane Darwaza )
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
385 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 134 0 R
/E (The alternative entry is via the Gunjavane route, approached from Margasani on the Nasrapur Velhe road, crossing the Kanadi and Gunjavani rivers. The ascent is extremely steep, with narrow stairways and foothold niches carved directly into the rock face where the terrain becomes near unscalable. The Gunjavane Darwaza consists of three successive gateways. The first is architecturally plain, with strong flanking bastions and remains of a timber door approximately 4.5 metres high. The second gate, around 17 metres beyond, features an intricately carved sculpture, possibly depicting Maya Devi or Lakshmi, though this is unlikely to date from Shivaji's era given the absence of similar carvings at his other forts. The limited defensive features of this gateway suggest it originates from an earlier period, possibly under the Rashtrakutas or Western Chalukyas. Small guardrooms flank the interior of the second gate. The third gateway survives only in a ruined state, beyond which one enters the Padmavati Machi.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
