/Title (The most striking feature of Suvela Machi is the eastern ridge, approached via the commanding cylindrical Tehalni Buruj \(Watch Bastion\), bastion B25, which rises 12 metres and is built upon a natural rock formation resembling a colossal east-facing elephant, locally known as the Hatti Prastar. Within this formation is the famous Nedhe or Needle Hole Rock, a natural cavity window popular among trekkers and climbers. The second segment culminates in bastion B26, the eastern sentinel of Rajgad, featuring double tiers of fortification built onto the rocky outcrop, though now significantly deteriorated. Both segments of Suvela Machi feature outer parakotas \(protective walls\) with jharokhas \(windows\) for offensive use, along with an underground bhuyari parakota \(underground passage\) set half a metre inward from the outer wall, connected to the exterior by a hidden stairway allowing soldiers to reach firing positions safely. Water management was central to Suvela Machi's strategic planning. Several major cisterns, designated W15 to W19, were constructed to sustain the garrison along the spur. One of these, WT16, is locally known as the Brahmarishi Kunda and doubles as a Rameshwar Temple, housing a Shivalinga, a broken Nandi \(bull\), a female idol, and a male figure possibly representing Veerbhadra or a Dvarapala \(door guardian\).)
/Dest [30 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1233 0 R
